WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.580
Today we're back with unintended consequences, episode 61 for July 17th, 2026.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:11.100
Friday, let's wrap the week with a story that has a real lesson in it.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.120
The Royal Navy cured Skurvy with lemon juice in 1795, then quietly lost the remedy as

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:24.800
steamships and limes replaced the original practice. In April 1912, on the Ross ice shelf,

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:29.440
the last supporting party turned back from Robert Falcon Scott's polar party.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.300
They watched the men continue south, already showing the telltale stiffness and bleeding gums of

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:41.720
Skurvy. The expedition carried standardized lime juice, issued by the Royal Navy, yet the

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:47.980
disease still appeared, a cure that had once made long voyages safe had become unreliable without

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:54.300
anyone noticing the change. What stands out here is how the symptoms emerged on a journey whose

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.380
planners had every reason to believe the official ration would prevent them.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:04.040
The stiffness in the joints and the bleeding gums were the same signs that had once empty decks

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:11.060
on sailing ships, yet the men had followed the standing orders without deviation. In 1747,

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:17.400
Scottish naval surgeon James Lind conducted his classic trial aboard HMS Salisbury.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.100
He gave sailors suffering from Skurvy either seawater, vinegar, or citrus fruit.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:29.180
The two men who received oranges and lemons recovered within days while the others did not.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:36.780
Lind's 1753 treatise on the Skurvy recommended fresh citrus as the most effective remedy then known.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:43.340
By the 1780s, physician Gilbert Blaine, serving as commissioner to the sick and hurtboard,

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:47.360
pressed the admiralty to adopt lemon juice as standard issue.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.940
The decision addressed a problem that had killed more sailors than combat.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:58.160
On some 18th century fleets, Skurvy claimed one-third of a crew before a voyage ended.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:04.220
If a ship sailed with 300 men, that meant roughly 100 lives lost to the disease alone

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:10.080
before any shot was fired. Blaine and his colleagues were working with the evidence available,

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:15.700
repeated shipboard demonstrations that citrus worked, rather than any understanding of vitamins.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.800
They could point to the men who had walked again after a few days of oranges and lemons,

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:25.880
and that observable recovery was enough to justify changing the supply lists.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:34.220
In 1795, the admiralty ordered lemon juice issued daily to all royal navy ships on foreign stations.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:41.880
By 1800, the order applied fleet-wide, surgeons recorded the juice in their logs as anti-scorbutic,

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.720
and the incidents of Skurvy dropped sharply on long deployments.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.340
The measure coincided with Britain's growing naval dominance during the Napoleonic Wars,

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.400
reinforcing the perception that the policy was sound.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:03:00.280
Early success was measured simply by the absence of the disease on vessels that previously lost

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:06.460
dozens of men. No laboratory existed to test the active principle, so the navy preserved the

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:12.240
practice as a standing order rather than a scientific theory. One way to see the strength of

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:18.420
that decision is to note how the daily ration became part of the normal routine. Each man received

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:24.560
his portion at the same time the water was issued, and the logs simply stopped listing Skurvy cases

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:31.660
where they had once filled pages. Steam propulsion shortened typical voyage length after the 1840s,

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:36.380
and Skurvy became rare enough that routine observation of its prevention faded.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:42.340
The navy shifted its citrus supply from Sicilian lemons to cheaper West Indian limes,

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.200
whose juice contained roughly one-third the vitamin C of lemons.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:52.740
If a daily lemon ration had once delivered on the order of 30 to 40 milligrams of the active compound,

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:58.280
the lime ration now supplied perhaps 10 to 15 milligrams under the same volume.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:04.000
The juice was stored for months in copper vats that further reduced potency through oxidation

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:10.700
and metal contamination. Copper ions speed the breakdown of the compound when air is present,

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.180
and the vats were opened and resealed repeatedly during long storage.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:21.400
Because no sailor developed obvious Skurvy on the shorter routes, the weaker preparation appeared

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:28.000
equally effective. The absence of new cases on the steam-powered runs served as confirmation that

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:34.540
the system still worked even though the material inside the casks had changed. When longer expeditions

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:42.440
resumed in the polar regions, the same lime juice failed to protect crews. Scotts 1901 to 1904

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:49.580
and 1910 to 1912 expeditions both recorded scorebutic symptoms despite following the official ration.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:56.040
Medical officers attributed the cases to tainted tinned meat or domain poisoning rather than

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:02.140
insufficient vitamin C. Without knowledge of the underlying mechanism, the Navy could not recognize

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:08.000
that its institutional memory had preserved a procedure whose active ingredient had quietly disappeared.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:14.060
The men on the ice followed every step that had been written down yet the chain that once connected

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:18.980
the fruit on the dock to the health of the crew had been altered at two separate points.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:25.380
The choice of fruit and the method of storage vitamin C was isolated in 1932,

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:31.140
allowing researchers to confirm that the original lemon juice had supplied an essential nutrient

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:38.640
the later lime juice did not. The admiralty revised its anti-scorbutic provisions in the 1930s,

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:43.540
but the episode left no formal inquiry into how a working remedy had been degraded.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:49.340
Modern navies and Antarctic programs now test ascorbic acid levels directly.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:54.960
The episode remains a reference point in discussions of institutional knowledge loss whenever

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:00.680
procedures outlive the conditions that made them reliable. When a remedy succeeds without an

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:06.600
explanation of why it works, any later change in materials, storage, or operating conditions can

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:12.620
erode its effectiveness without immediate detection. Organizations therefore need mechanisms that

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:18.720
preserve the underlying mechanism, not merely the checklist. The same risk appears today whenever

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:25.160
automated systems or regulatory routines replace earlier practices whose original rationale has been

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:31.480
forgotten. How many current safety or health protocols rest on results that would no longer hold

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:37.820
if the hidden variables shifted? And that's where today's story leaves us. If it changed how you see

